I'm thrilled to welcome back to the  program Terry Cogan who's professor of  law at the SJ Quinney College of Law at  the University of Utah uh Terry it's so  great to talk to you you know we've been  following these uh the back-and-forth  catalyzed by a series of three tweets by  President Trump about his intentions to  ban all transgender military service  members from service of any kind and  aside from the reality that the Pentagon  has said hey we don't do things based on  tweets and only when we're actually  directed by the president formally to do  this are we going to start doing it I've  had some serious both legal and  logistical questions about this and sort  of the first one I think might be  interesting to talk about is how do you  go about enforcing such a ban as  proposed by the president well the  question becomes whether in fact one can  adopt formal military policy in three  tweets ah the military the Pentagon the  Joint Chiefs of Staff US Secretary of  Defense mattis seem to be taking the  position that nothing no new policy  change has occurred until the military  gets direct instructions from the White  House there are a few legal experts who  in interesting articles in the last  several days have argued that a tweet  from the president is as forceful as a  formal statement but what is important  is that our military appears at this  point to be sitting back and waiting and  doing nothing to change its policy um  with respect to transgender people  serving in the military until it gets  further direction to the White House I  sort of understand if you're talking  about incoming military service members  you know you you there is a medical exam  that is done and presumably a doctor  could determine if you know and  enlistees genitals don't match the  gender that was selected  sex that was selected on their  application for example but with regard  to the you know two to six thousand  transgender individuals that are already  in the military what practical way would  there be to enforce such a ban and would  that raise any legal questions so let me  step back I'm David and let's make clear  that the traditional vision which is  really the one your adverting to that  transgender people are those who have  undergone sex change operation in fact  is not the current reality correct that  in fact there are some transgender  people previously called transsexuals  who have in fact undergone major surgery  to align their bodies with their gender  identities which are opposite from that  of their birth bodies but there are many  transgender people who choose to deal  with their gender identity in different  ways perhaps with just hormones or some  with no hormones perhaps with just  bodily presentation so the idea that you  can do a medical exam and discover what  it is transgender in fact is not the  case so let's start off with that  secondly and this is the thrust I think  the importance of your question in fact  based on a study done by the Williams  Institute the estimate that the Williams  Institute at UCLA came up with is that  there currently are about 15,000  transgender people working for the  military 6,000 of whom are in active  duty which accords with the number you  came up with which really came from the  RAND Corporation correct and the  question becomes what do you do with all  of these people do you basically kick  them out as a number of people have  pointed out if the quote tremendous  medical cost that the press  and referred to is six to eight million  dollars the cost of replacing 15,000  workers for the military is massively  greater than the six to eight thousand  dollars that it is costing estimated for  transgender medical treatment at the  moment  that's fascinating now do we do we know  Terry what it's considered to cost two  for the full recruitment to active duty  of a service member off the top of my  head I do not what I have a read in the  last days if everyone agrees that the  amount to recruit the cost to reprove  15,000 new people is many multiples you  have the $18.00 got a debt it is costing  for transgender medical treatment  fascinating so that still would also not  answer one of the other really important  legal questions which is that of just  thinking ahead here right just plank  playing this along the to to its  conclusion honorable versus dishonorable  discharge there have been those who say  no no it would definitely be honorable  discharges but that alone is no panacea  during the times of Don't Ask Don't Tell  you could be honorably discharged with a  reason stated like for example  homosexual admission or something like  that and that is still potentially  really problematic for people in seeking  future employment etc so this offhanded  notion that don't worry if we did  identify the people and if the solution  was we say goodbye but it's an honorable  discharge that there's no no need to  worry that that's a great outcome that's  very problematic as well isn't it let me  preface it by saying I am by no means an  expert on military law so this in terms  of the criteria for honorable versus  dishonorable discharge were a bit out of  my realm um  having said that I think that the  repercussions of actually dismissing  15,000 transgender people who are  serving our military is far broader than  just on those 15,000 people arguably in  in contradiction with the suggestions  that the White House was has made that  somehow this is necessary for combat  readiness there is a strong belief that  this would be one of the more  demoralizing things that the  administration could do in terms of  military readiness that the impact on  exists on other non transgender military  people who are in units with transgender  people who are getting along fine right  now  would have a terrible depressing impact  on a military moreover given that our  generation of Millennials those who are  now considering military service are far  more open and accepting of human  diversity the idea that the military  dismisses 15,000 people could have a  terrific killing effect it seems to me  on the ability of the military to  recruit in the future it seems to me  Terry that while there are some who like  to say hey listen the issue of LGBT  rights it's sort of been resolved we had  the Supreme Court decision saying states  can no longer ban same-sex marriage this  is sort of an issue that we've gotten  past it seems like that's a drastic  misunderstanding of reality isn't it I  mean and I'm not talking just about the  dis latest decree from Donald Trump but  the reality that in many states you can  be fired just for your perceived or  actual sexual orientation the increased  use of so-called religious freedom to  justify discrimination against members  of the LGBT community there's still a  long way to go isn't there you were  absolutely right David that the fact  is that at this point it is still highly  contested as a legal matter as to  whether title seven the employment  anti-discrimination law protects LGBT  people from being fired simply for being  LGBT so one has seen this common quote  now that given the current state of the  law and given the Supreme Court's  marriage equality decision in obergefell  you can get married on Saturday night to  someone of the same sex and you can be  fired on Monday morning for having  gotten married on Saturday night so the  law is still very much up in the air  very importantly the Seventh Circuit  Federal Court of Appeals which is out of  Chicago and one of the most important  courts in the country has recently come  out with two very very important  decisions the first of which said that  title seven the anti-discrimination law  does cover discrimination against people  because of sexual orientation and then  in the decision roughly a month ago the  Court determined that under title 9  which is the law that protects students  are in public education I it in  particular it was a case dealing with a  another controversial issue involving  the right of transgender students to use  public restrooms in their schools that  accord with their gender identity the  Seventh Circuit determined that that  discriminating against a transgender  student by not allowing them to use the  restroom that accords with their gender  identity in fact is discrimination under  title 9 a very very important decision  for the transgender community so though  important courts are moving in this  direction  Premium Court has yet to weigh in  directly on this issue so it's very much  up in the air and the fact that there's  marriage equality by no means assures  that there's not employment  discrimination we will continue  following what how altom Utley happens  with this proposed transgender military  ban from President Trump we've been  speaking with Terry Cogan professor of  law as the at the SJ Quinney College of  Law at the University of Utah Terry  always great to talk to you good to talk  to you David

